{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789298292,"nonce":"hUWZA929uEWty/9nGhItbQbIjFM=","title":"Stub bridge logs an ordinary caught exception as a failure","message":"raise_stub_bridge_failure (crates/ash_interp/src/interpreter/mod.rs\n~2325) prints \"[ash] stub bridge: findex N failed, raising into the HL\ntrap chain: Uncaught exception: \u003cmsg\u003e\" once per findex, then hlp_throws\ninto the innermost trap. On the path its own comment describes (\"their\ncall_native boundary has an HL trap armed, so preserve normal Haxe\nexception semantics\") the raise succeeds and the exception is caught,\nso the line reports a failure that did not happen. Under caribou this is\nthe normal path for every Haxe exception that crosses to another\nlanguage through a typed call into interpreted code: a program that\nthrows and catches on purpose fills stderr with \"failed\".\n\nMove the line behind ASH_DBG (or the stub-bridge trace switch, if one\nexists) or drop it; keep it unconditional only for the genuinely fatal\nbranches in jit_stub_call_bridge (no interpreter context, worker lane\nwith an unprepared sentinel). Checked while there: the interpreter has\nunwound its own frames before the bridge sees the Err, and hlp_throw\nrestores exception state through the pooled trap contexts before the\nlongjmp, so no cleanup is skipped on this path.","files":null}]}